## 7. Known open items / risks carried forward

- **TLS 1.3 hybrid (X25519+ML-KEM-768)**: `docker/reverse-proxy.conf` is configured correctly but requires a non-stock nginx/OpenSSL build with the Open Quantum Safe `oqs-provider` — the plain `nginx:1.27-alpine` image does not support this out of the box. Deferred to Phase 16 (Security Hardening); documented in `DECISIONS.md`.
- **Frontend npm audit**: 5 advisories, all one `esbuild`→`vite`→`vitest` chain (dev-server-only issue, not a production runtime risk). Fixing requires a breaking Vite 5→8 jump, deliberately not forced yet. Tracked for Phase 16.
- **T046 (frontend auth pages) — DONE**: Register / Login / Verify-email / Home pages wired to the live `/api/v1/auth/*` contract, with the "cryptographic blueprint" design system. See `docs/phases/phase-04-frontend-report.md`. The session store currently persists the refresh token in `localStorage` as a dev convenience — production hardening (HttpOnly refresh cookie, in-memory-only access token, FR-005 rotation) is US10; the store interface is final so that swap is localized.
- **`TokenRepository`** is currently scoped to `EmailVerificationToken` only; Phase 9 (US7) will decide whether `PasswordResetToken` gets its own repository or extends this one.
- **Audit logging is a no-op stub** (T047): `AuditLogger` Protocol + `NoOpAuditLogger` are wired into all auth call sites; US11 swaps in the DB-backed `AuditService` without changing call sites.
- **Mailhog is now in `docker-compose.yml`** (service `mailhog`: SMTP `:1025`, web inbox `:8025`). With it running, the `send_verification_email` arq job delivers a real email — open **http://localhost:8025** to read it and click the verification link, instead of scraping the worker log. Verified end-to-end: register → worker logs `verification_email_sent` (SMTP succeeds) → email appears in the Mailhog inbox with the correct From/To/Subject. The host `.env`'s `SMTP_HOST=localhost:1025` already routes the host-running worker to it; the in-docker `worker` service overrides `SMTP_HOST=mailhog` to reach the container by name. Start it with `docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml --env-file .env up -d mailhog`.
- **Phase 5 (US2) — DONE (backend + frontend)**: identity-key directory (publish/list/rotate with real ML-DSA-65 attestation verification), direct conversations (self-conversation rejected, both participants authorized), opaque message send/list with `(sent_at, id)` cursor pagination, and the `/api/v1/ws` realtime endpoint (auth-at-upgrade, `message.send`→`message.new` fan-out). **Backend** stores/relays ciphertext + envelope verbatim and never parses content (FR-051/SC-002); `integrity_tag_valid_on_receipt` is always False server-side — full authenticity is the client's job (FR-027). **Frontend** does all content crypto in-browser: `conversationCrypto.ts` establishes one ML-KEM-768 shared secret per conversation → HKDF-SHA3-256 → a reused ChaCha20-Poly1305 message key, sealing each message with an ML-DSA-65 signature over `concat(ciphertext, nonce, aad)` (verify-then-decrypt on receipt). A full Double Ratchet is a documented MVP deferral (changes only `conversationCrypto.ts`; server is content-agnostic). The Conversations page is a two-pane thread with realtime ingest; plaintext lives only in an in-memory side-cache, never persisted (FR-051). Tests: backend 69 passed (10 new US2, real liboqs), ruff/mypy --strict(65 files)/black/bandit clean; frontend 30 passed (6 new crypto round-trips with real @noble post-quantum + 8 messagingService), tsc/lint/build clean. See `docs/phases/phase-05-messaging-backend-report.md` + `phase-05-messaging-frontend-report.md`.
- Every other identified gap from the two `/speckit-analyze` passes has been fixed (see `tasks.md`'s lettered remediation tasks: T006a, T051a, T059a, T059b, T061a, T128a, T128b, T128c).
- **Phase 5b (Username Discovery) — DONE**: the username is now the unique public identifier. Signup collects Email + Username + Password (FR-052; `display_name` is retained but defaults to the username, editable later via US7). After login the UI shows the logged-in user's username (pulled from `/users/me` via `authStore.loadProfile()` — the JWT still carries only `sub`/`sid`, so this is the correct non-workaround flow). The New Conversation box takes a **username**, resolves it through the authenticated user directory (`GET /users/search` → exact, case-insensitive, rate-limited, **no email**) and starts the direct conversation with the resolved user id via the existing `POST /conversations` — no manual session ids or copying (FR-053). Migration `0003_username_baseline` backfills existing rows from the email local-part. `/users/{id}` returns a public summary (username + display_name, no email); `/users/me` is the only view with email. Gates: backend pytest 87 passed, ruff/mypy --strict(68 files)/bandit/black clean, alembic at 0003; frontend tsc/eslint/vitest 41 passed/build clean. See `docs/phases/phase-05-username-discovery-report.md`.
- **Dual PostgreSQL on :5432 (environment cleanup, not a code bug)**: a native PostgreSQL 18 on the host shadows the Docker `postgres` container on `127.0.0.1:5432`, so the app talks to the native instance (see "Current infrastructure state" above). This works but is confusing. Recommended cleanup (pick one, not urgent): (a) stop/disable the native PG18 service and use the Docker container exclusively — then run `alembic upgrade head` against the Docker container to (re)create the schema; or (b) keep native PG18 and stop the Docker `postgres` service / remap its host port (e.g. `5433`) so there is a single source of truth. Either way, keep `.env`'s `DATABASE_URL` password in sync with the chosen instance's `vayunx` user.

## 8. Rules that must not be silently relaxed

From `CONSTITUTION.md` / `.specify/memory/constitution.md` — the two that are easiest to accidentally violate mid-implementation:

- **No hardcoded secrets, keys, IVs, salts, or certificates** anywhere in source. Test fixtures use clearly-labeled dev-only generated values (e.g. `backend/tests/fixtures/`), never committed production secrets.
- **RBAC/authorization must be enforced at the service layer**, not only at the router/middleware layer — relevant starting Phase 4/5.

## 9. If you're a new session picking this up

Read, in order: this file → `CONSTITUTION.md` → `specs/001-e2ee-chat-platform/spec.md` (skim) → `tasks.md` (skim the phase you're about to start) → the most recent `docs/phases/phase-NN-*-report.md`. Then ask the user for explicit approval to start the next phase — do not assume it's already been given.

